Continual Learning: To detect concept drift and continuously adapt to new attack behaviors while retaining knowledge of previously learned attack classes.
Incremental Learning: To learn new attack classes or newly collected blockchain data without retraining the entire model from scratch.
Lifelong Learning: To accumulate knowledge from historical attack patterns and leverage it when encountering future attacks.
Class-Incremental Learning: To add new attack categories (e.g., new scam types) over time while preserving performance on existing classes.
Task-Incremental Learning: To sequentially learn different blockchain security tasks such as phishing detection, Ponzi detection, and rug-pull detection.
Domain-Incremental Learning: To adapt a model trained on one blockchain ecosystem (e.g., Ethereum) to another domain (e.g., BNB Chain, Polygon) without forgetting previous knowledge.
Online Learning: To update the model in real time as new transactions and smart contract interactions arrive.
Experience Replay Learning: To store representative samples of past attacks and replay them during training to mitigate catastrophic forgetting.
Memory-Augmented Learning: To utilize an external memory mechanism that preserves important attack patterns and historical transaction behaviors.
Knowledge Distillation: To transfer knowledge from a large, complex model to a smaller model while retaining attack detection performance.
Graph Continual Learning: To continuously learn from evolving blockchain transaction graphs while maintaining knowledge of previous graph structures.
Incremental Graph Learning: To update graph representations when new nodes, edges, or transaction relationships appear in the blockchain network.
Open-World Learning: To recognize previously unseen attack types and flag them as unknown rather than forcing classification into existing categories.
Concept Drift Learning: To handle changes in attacker behavior, transaction patterns, and smart contract exploitation techniques over time.
Continual Federated Learning: To continuously learn from distributed blockchain data sources while preserving privacy and adapting to evolving threats.
Transfer Learning: To transfer knowledge learned from one blockchain dataset or attack type to another related detection task.
Meta-Learning: To enable rapid adaptation to newly emerging attacks using only a small number of labeled examples.
Curriculum Learning: To improve training by gradually introducing attack samples from simple patterns to more complex attack scenarios.
Active Learning: To reduce labeling costs by selecting the most informative blockchain transactions for expert annotation.
Self-Supervised Learning: To learn meaningful transaction and address representations from unlabeled blockchain data.
Semi-Supervised Learning: To utilize both labeled and large-scale unlabeled blockchain transactions for improved attack classification.
Few-Shot Learning: To detect rare attack categories using only a handful of labeled examples.
Zero-Shot Learning: To identify attack types that were never seen during training using semantic knowledge and learned representations.
Multi-Task Learning: To jointly learn multiple security tasks such as attack classification, anomaly detection, and fraud detection.
Federated Learning: To train models across multiple organizations or blockchain monitoring systems without sharing raw data.
Adaptive Learning: To dynamically adjust model parameters according to changing blockchain environments and threat landscapes.
Representation Learning: To automatically learn effective feature embeddings from transaction graphs and smart contract interactions.
Contrastive Learning: To learn discriminative embeddings by pulling similar transaction behaviors together and pushing different behaviors apart.
Graph Neural Network Learning: To capture complex transaction relationships and network structures for attack detection.
Streaming Learning: To process and learn from continuous streams of blockchain transactions in real time.
Open-Set Recognition Learning: To identify whether a transaction belongs to a known attack category or represents an unseen threat.
Dynamic Architecture Learning: To expand or modify the neural network architecture when new attack patterns emerge.
Prototype-Based Learning: To classify attacks based on representative prototypes of each attack class.
Self-Evolving Learning: To autonomously update the model as blockchain ecosystems and attacker strategies evolve.
Concept Drift-Aware Learning: To explicitly monitor and adapt to distribution shifts in transaction behavior over time.
Graph Representation Learning: To generate low-dimensional embeddings that preserve structural information from blockchain transaction graphs.
Continual Graph Learning: To continuously update graph-based knowledge as blockchain networks evolve without forgetting previous graph patterns.
Anomaly Detection Learning: To identify suspicious transactions and smart contracts that deviate from normal blockchain behavior.
Domain Adaptation: To transfer attack detection knowledge between different blockchain platforms with differing transaction characteristics.
Domain Generalization: To build models that generalize well to unseen blockchain networks and environments without retraining.
